We develop an enhanced Monte Carlo method for simulating quantum circuits by breaking down complex unitary operations into more fundamental components, specifically single-qubit rotations and controlled SWAP gates. This approach streamlines the simulation process, reducing the computational cost of the randomized sparsification algorithm from $\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(2^n)$ to $\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(n^3)$ for simulating circuits comprising $n$ qubits. This improvement is achieved by leveraging the structure of the unitary decomposition to create a more efficient sparsification, thereby circumventing the exponential complexity associated with the original method. Furthermore, our algorithm is adaptable to various types of quantum hardware and can be integrated with existing simulation tools. Its implementation is compatible with the Qiskit Terra simulation package, allowing it to be seamlessly incorporated into existing workflows. This enhanced algorithm is showcased through several numerical demonstrations, including simulations of the Quantum Volume benchmark and the Real-Time Variational Quantum Simulator. Moreover, we demonstrate the feasibility of our algorithm on a conventional computing system, specifically the IBM ZH1N and ZU2L nodes, which are equipped with quantum hardware. This achievement paves the way for the practical application of randomized sparsification in quantum circuit simulation. The accompanying code for the enhanced algorithm is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/IBM/Qiskit-Terra/pull/6461. This research was supported by IBM Research. The authors acknowledge the support of their respective institutions and funding agencies. The authors also acknowledge the support of their respective institutions and funding agencies. The authors also acknowledge the support of their respective institutions and funding agencies. The authors also acknowledge the support of their respective institutions and funding agencies. 
