Pretrained language models have demonstrated impressive performance in various video classification tasks when fine-tuned on limited labeled data. However, their high computational requirements during the transfer process can hinder their practical application in mobile devices. To mitigate this issue, we propose a novel approach called Efficient Transfer Learning for Video Classification (EVL4VC). This method leverages the pretraining process to extract discriminative features from video segments and reduces the complexity of fine-tuning, thereby eliminating the need for redundant computations. As a result, we achieve a substantial 80% reduction in FLOPs compared to traditional fine-tuning methods, while maintaining comparable performance on benchmark datasets. Moreover, our model can efficiently execute on mobile devices, outperforming other lightweight models by a significant margin. The accompanying code is publicly available at https://github.com/zhouchengyue/EVL4VC. Prof. Haifeng He and Prof. Xing Xu provided valuable guidance and support from Peking University. The research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant No. 62106299. A patent has been filed for this innovative approach. The authors declare no conflict of interest. The research was also supported by the Artificial Intelligence Institute of the Ministry of Education of the People's Republic of China. \textbf{This work has been submitted to IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Letters, but it has not been officially accepted.} \vspace{-0.1cm} \vspace{\vspace{\vspace{\vspace{\vspace{}}}}} 
