This paper investigates the impact of phase noise on conditional bit-error-rate (BER) performance of a generalized union of modulations (GUM) scheme, which combines multiple modulations. The performance is evaluated for a GUM scheme with $N$ modulations, each operating at a binary BER, to establish the minimum required signal-to-noise ratio for successful transmission. Specifically, this study focuses on the GUM scheme in the presence of phase noise, a common occurrence in wireless channels. The results show that the performance of the GUM scheme is influenced by the average phase noise, which can be used to determine the optimal modulation combination for a given BER. Interestingly, this study reveals that the GUM scheme with a single modulation (BPSK) performs as well as the combination of all modulations (QPSK) for a specific average phase noise. The findings of this paper can be utilized to develop optimal modulation strategies for wireless communication systems. The performance analysis presented in this study is validated through simulation results. The outcomes of this paper can be applied to develop optimal modulation strategies for wireless communication systems. The corresponding code is available at https://github.com/ahmed-ahm/PhaseNoiseGUM. \end{note} 

